\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces Approximate convex decomposition. (a) ACD evaluates the concavity $c(P)$ of each polygonal component. If the concavity is below the prescribed threshold, the component is accepted; otherwise, the dominant reflex notch $r$ is resolved to produce $P_1$ and $P_2$, which are processed recursively. (b) The dashed boundary $H_P$ denotes the convex hull used to measure the concavity at $r$; successive cuts decompose $P$ into smaller approximately convex components. The symbol $\tau $ in panel~(a) denotes the local ACD tolerance. \cite  {LienAmato2006}}}{4}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:acd-step}{{1}{4}{}{}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsection}{\numberline {2.3}Visibility Clique Cover}{4}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{sec:vcc}{{2.3}{4}{}{}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces Visibility Clique Cover pipeline. A visibility graph is constructed from collision-free samples and covered by groups of mutually visible vertices. Each visibility clique initializes an ellipsoidal seed, which is expanded by IRIS-style inflation into a collision-free convex polytope. Sampling, clique construction, and region inflation are repeated to improve free-space coverage, producing the final collection of overlapping convex regions.}}{5}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:vcc-pipeline}{{2}{5}{}{}{}}
